.TH CLOGGER 7 "2026-10-06" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Overview"
.SH NAME
clogger \- overview of the structured logger
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/clogger.h>
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
The module writes structured log records.  A record is a sequence of
key=value pairs, in this order:
  - a fixed header with the time, the level, the process, the thread
    and the source location;
  - the persistent fields of the logger;
  - the message.
There are three formats: logfmt (the default), NDJSON and RFC 5424
syslog; see clog_set_format(3).

A logger writes to one of these outputs:
  - a file descriptor that the caller owns (clog_create_from_fd_mp(3));
  - a file that the logger opens itself (clog_create_from_file_mp(3)),
    with optional size rotation, time rotation and gzip compression of
    the rotated files.
Either kind of logger can log synchronously, or hand its writes to a
writer thread of its own (async mode, through a clog_async_cfg_t).
.fi
.SS Handles
.nf
A clog is an opaque value handle, a uint64_t, and not a pointer, so
never cast it to or from void *.  CLOG_NULL, which is 0, means "no
logger", and the open functions and clog_derive(3) give it on failure.
You can test for it with if (!lg) or with lg == CLOG_NULL.

Every function that takes a handle needs a live one.  Given
CLOG_NULL, a handle whose logger is closed or a stale handle, it
stops the process with a diagnostic, and such a handle is never ignored
silently.

clog_derive(3) makes a second handle on the same output.  A root logger
and its derived loggers share the output target, the format, the syslog
facility, the rotation, the async writer thread, the allocator and the
lock that serialises the writes, while each handle has a minimum level
and fields of its own.
.fi
.SS Levels
.nf
  CLOG_TRACE  0  The finest detail.
  CLOG_DEBUG  1
  CLOG_INFO   2  The usual minimum in production.
  CLOG_WARN   3
  CLOG_ERROR  4  Adds a backtrace.
  CLOG_ALERT  5  An operator must act immediately (RFC 5424 severity
                 1).  Adds a backtrace.
  CLOG_FATAL  6  Adds a backtrace, then stops the process with
                 exit(EXIT_FAILURE).  Ignores the minimum level.
  CLOG_OFF    7  As a minimum level, stops all output except
                 CLOG_FATAL.

A record below the minimum level of the logger is dropped silently.
Each of the macros clog_trace(3) to clog_fatal(3) logs at one level,
and clog_set_level(3) changes the minimum at any time.
.fi
.SS Record contents
.nf
The proc key has the form <name>(<pid>):<tname>(<tid>).  The first
part is the basename of the executable with the process ID, and the
second is the name and the ID of the thread that logged.  After
ccol_set_thread_name(3) renames a thread, its records show the new
name.

In logfmt, a field value or a message goes in double quotes when it
holds a space, an '=', a '"', a backslash, a control character or a
byte that is not well-formed UTF-8, and those characters are escaped
with a backslash (\e", \e\e, \en, \er, \et, \exNN, or \eu0080 to
\eu009f for a C1 control), so a message or a value can never split a
record across lines.  In every format each byte that is not part of a
well-formed UTF-8 sequence becomes U+FFFD, which keeps every record
valid UTF-8.  JSON and syslog have their own escapes, described in
clog_set_format(3) and clog_set_field(3).

A record has a hard size limit of 16 MiB, and the library never writes
a truncated or malformed record.  A record above the limit is replaced
with a well-formed record in the same format that keeps the header and
says "log record too large to emit", and an allocation failure while a
record is built gives a record that says "log record dropped", so a
record never disappears without a trace.  A "log record truncated: ..."
record at CLOG_ERROR names the records that a write could not deliver.
If the output refuses that record as well, as a full disk does, the
logger writes it in front of the next write that the output accepts, at
the next flush of an async logger, or at exit when nothing is written
again, and it never writes a record onto the fragment that a cut write
left; see clog_create_from_fd_mp(3).
.fi
.SS Backtraces
.nf
clog_error(3), clog_alert(3) and clog_fatal(3) add a backtrace, which
goes out as continuation lines with a tab indent in logfmt, as a "bt"
array in JSON and as one message per frame in syslog.  Link the program
with -rdynamic to get symbol names.  The capture works on platforms
with execinfo.h (glibc, FreeBSD, macOS), and when it fails a marker
takes the place of the frames ("#error backtrace unavailable" in
logfmt, a "bt_error" key in JSON), so a missing backtrace is always
visible.
.fi
.SS Rotation
.nf
A rotating logger renames, compresses and deletes only its own rotated
generations, and clog_create_from_file_mp(3) gives the exact test.  A
name whose timestamp is more than 30 days above both the clock and the
newest name the logger made is not a generation, so the logger never
renames, compresses or deletes it, and a CLOG_WARN record names it.
When the system clock steps back by more than 30 days, a logger that
opens the file after the step therefore leaves the generations written
before the step unmanaged until the clock comes back within 30 days of
their timestamps; until then the operator removes or renames them.
.fi
.SS Memory management
.nf
clog_create_from_fd_mp(3) and clog_create_from_file_mp(3) take an
optional ccol_memmgmt_procs_t, and the root logger and every logger
derived from it allocate the shared output state, the write buffers,
the message buffers and the fields through it.  Two allocations belong
to the library and not to one logger, so they don't go through it: the
symbol text of a backtrace, which backtrace_symbols(3) allocates with
malloc(3), and the small identity cache (program name, process and
thread ID, thread name) that each thread fills with malloc(3) on its
first record.  Every logger of the process shares that cache, and it is
freed when the thread exits.  All four function pointers must be set.
The library copies the struct, so you can free yours after the open.
.fi
.SS Thread safety
.nf
Every function can be called from many threads at the same time on
handles that stay open.  One lock serialises the writes and the
rotation of an output target, so the lines of two threads never mix
and a backtrace always follows its own record.  The level and the
fields of a logger are synchronised apart from that lock.

clog_close(3) is the exception, as it is for every handle type of the
library: while it runs, no other thread may use or close the same
handle.  Closing one handle while a sibling handle (the root or another
derived logger) logs is fine.
.fi
.SS Process exit
.nf
The library registers an exit handler when it loads.  When the process
ends with exit(3) or a return from main(), that handler writes the
records that each async logger holds and completes the queued
compressions, all within a budget of 5 seconds, and clog_fatal(3) uses
the same drain.  _exit(2), a signal and abort(3) run no exit handler,
so if the queued records matter, call clog_flush(3) or clog_close(3)
first.  clog_create_from_fd_mp(3) has the details.
.fi
.SS fork(2)
.nf
There are two supported ways to use fork(2) with this module:
  - call fork(2) before you open a logger;
  - open a logger, and then call fork(2) and exec(3) immediately.
A child that goes on without exec(3) while a logger from before the
fork is live is not supported.

As a defence in depth, an inherited handle still works in such a child.
An async logger switches to synchronous writes there, because its
writer thread does not exist in the child.  This fallback is part of
the fork protection of the library, which a build with
-DCCOL_FORK_SAFETY_REQUIRED=0 leaves out, and a process from such a
build must not fork while it holds a live async logger.
.fi
.SS SIGPIPE and SIGXFSZ
.nf
A write to a pipe or a socket whose reader is gone causes SIGPIPE, and
a write to a regular file at the file size limit of the process
(RLIMIT_FSIZE) causes SIGXFSZ.  With the default disposition either
signal ends the process.  By default the library keeps both signals
from ending the process; for SIGXFSZ, the open of a regular file sets
its disposition to SIG_IGN while it is still SIG_DFL.
clog_set_signal_policy(3) describes how, and also the policy that
leaves both signals to the application, which then has to ignore
SIGXFSZ or block it in every thread that can write a log file.
.fi
